                <pb facs="0226" n="124" sameAs="frus:frus1908/pg124"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 16576/4–6.</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Consul McNally to the
                            Assistant Secretary of State.</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >American Consulate</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                            >Nanking</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian" when="1909-02-09"
                                ><hi rendition="#italic">February 9, 1909</hi></date>.</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 90.]</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: Referring to that part of the department’s
                    dispatch No. 520, of November 30, 1908, to Mr. Rockhill, the minister at Peking,
                    which reads: “No copy of the regulations of the international settlement at Wuhu
                    has ever been received by the department,” I have the honor to inclose herewith
                    a translation of the regulations and a letter from T’ung Taotai at Wuhu to the
                    British consul at that port on the subject.</p>
                <p>I have, etc.,</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">J. C. McNally.</hi></signed></closer>



                <xi:include href="d143-a1.xml" xpointer="d143-a1"/>
